The strongest hadronic constraint is put on LHAASO J2226+6057 with a limit of \\lesssim 47\\%, with the assumption that the neutrino spectrum follows a simple power law with an index of 3.01 reported by LHAASO. The UHE source may be associated with SNR G106.3+02.7, suggested as a potential PeVatron by Albert et al. (2020) and Amenomori et al. (2021). The observations of HAWC and Tibet AS\\gamma for the SNR indicate an unbroken hard TeV spectrum extends up to at least 100 TeV. Amenomori et al. (2021) suggested that the VHE protons might be accelerated and trapped in the SNR given the hard spectral index. On the other hand, the UHE \\gamma-ray flux of J2226+6057 detected by LHAASO from 20 to 500 TeV is better described with log-parabola rather than a single power-law (Cao et al., 2021). However, the hadronic component of J2226+6057's \\gamma-ray flux cannot be constrained when applying the log-parabola model to calculate the neutrino flux limits.

For LHAASO J0534+2202, which is the UHE counterpart of the Crab Nebula, we constrain the hadronic UHE \\gamma-ray emission to \\lesssim 59\\% of the total, assuming a power law of \\Gamma=3.12 as reported by LHAASO. Applying the log-parabola model by HAWC, the hadronic constraint on the Crab is \\lesssim 84\\%, compared to \\lesssim 70\\% reported previously by Huang and Li (2021). Although a 1.12 PeV \\gamma-induced shower was detected by LHAASO-KM2A from the direction of the Crab, the hadronic origin of TeV \\gamma-rays from it is considered to be less likely (Cao et al., 2021). If the UHE emission from the Crab is hadronic, CRs should be accelerated in the pulsar's magnetosphere or at the wind termination shock. An alternative scenario proposed by Cheng et al. (1990) suggests that the CRs could be accelerated in the outer gap of the pulsar.

We could only marginally constrain the hadronic fraction of the \\gamma-ray flux for LHAASO J1849-0003 to \\lesssim 94\\%, with a power-law index of 1.99 (Huang and Li, 2021).

## 5 Conclusions and Outlook

In this study, we have performed catalog and stacking searches on twelve LHAASO UHE \\gamma-ray sources. We have found no significant neutrino emission, with the smallest post-trial p-value of 0.06 in PWN equal-weight stacking search. The resulting 90% neutrino flux upper limits for an E^{-2} spectrum are given. For LHAASO J1849-0003, LHAASO J0534+2202, and LHAASO J2226+6057, we are able to constrain the hadronic \\gamma-ray emission tailored to the available spectral information of these sources. Joint fits between \\gamma-rays and neutrinos (Fan et al., 2021) and physics-motivated lepto-hadronic modeling of the spectrum of each individual source may provide a better and more accurate limit. Future detectors should also improve our understanding of these potential PeVatrons. The planned IceCube-Gen2 detector (Aartsen et al., 2021), with an increased effective area relative to IceCube and improved angular resolution, is expected to become up to five times more sensitivity for Galactic neutrino sources compared to IceCube.

