## Appendix C Connection between Gamma-ray Flux and Neutrino Flux

In pp interactions, protons interact with nearby material and produce charged and neutral pions with a ratio of about 2 to 1 (Ahlers & Murase, 2014). Charged pion decay to neutrinos and other products, and the resulting neutrinos carry roughly 1/4 the energy of the pion. Neutral pion decay into two \\gamma-rays, each with 1/2 of the energy (Ahlers & Murase, 2014). On Earth, the flux observed is

E_{\\gamma}J_{\\gamma}(E_{\\gamma})\\approx e^{\\frac{-d}{\\gamma_{\\gamma}}}\\frac{1}{3 }\\sum_{\\nu_{\\alpha}}E_{\\nu}J_{\\nu_{\\alpha}}(E_{\\nu}), (C8) where J_{\\gamma}(E_{\\gamma}) and J_{\\nu_{\\alpha}}(E_{\\nu}) are the gamma-ray differential particle flux and neutrino differential particle flux for neutrino favor \\alpha. Assuming the absorption of gamma-rays is negligible and equal flux from each neutrino flavor due to oscillations, relationship between \\gamma-rayand neutrino fluxes is then

J_{\\nu_{\\mu}}(E_{\\nu_{\\mu}})=2J_{\\gamma}(E_{\\nu_{\\mu}}/2). (C9)